  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 243 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: GOES-19 visible satellite depicts a narrow moisture \ncorridor associated with a weak cold front stretched across the \nregion this afternoon. While most of the radar returns have ended up \nbeing virga, some locations in the St. Lawrence Valley have been \nable to pick up a few hundreths of an inch of rainfall. By and large \nhowever, many locations will remain dry with little to no measurable \nrain from this frontal system, especially those in Vermont where \ndewpoint depressions are 10-15 degrees. A few places along the \nnorthwest facing slopes of the Adirondacks and northern Greens, \nincluding Jay Peak, could a few sprinkles though due to orographic \ninfluences. Winds will turn to the north-northwest as the front \nmoves through this afternoon advecting sharply colder air and some \nbriefly gusty winds this evening from Canada. As nocturnal cooling \nbegins after sunset this evening, winds will briefly increase in the \nnorthern Champlain Valley up to 25 MPH or so between 7-10 PM. Winds \nwill weaken towards calm thereafter. Tonights lows will cool to the \nupper 20s for many locations with a few locations hovering around \nthe freezing mark in the wider valleys; coldest locations will be \nacross northern New York and the Northeast Kingdom. Tomorrow will \nfeature sunny skies with continued cool high temperatures struggling \nto reach the upper 40s. The coldest night of the season arrives \nTuesday night under strong radiational cooling. Overnight lows may \napproach some records for Montpelier and St. Johnsbury, with records \nelsewhere residing in the 10th percentile or lower. Temperatures \nwill drop to the teens across the Adirondacks and low 20s elsewhere. \nTemperatures slowly rebound Wednesday as high pressure and south \nwinds help to moderate our pattern. Temperatures will warm to the \nlow to mid 50s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Low chances for some showers on Thursday night as a \nfront crosses our area. With center of the low passing well north of \nour area, not a lot of strong forcing with this frontal passage. If \nwe have precipitation, it will be light. Then we once again expect \nmostly dry and seasonably warm weather for Friday and Saturday as a \nridge of surface high pressure builds into the north country. Some \nmodels indicate increasing chances for showers early next week, but \nnot a lot of model consistency in that timeframe at this point. \n.",
